{#
    The country box and the province box every admin screen shares. Include it rather than copying
    it — and never render a free-text province or country input again.

    It lives in core for the reason `product_field.html.twig` does: `config/bundles.php` discovers
    modules with glob() and `bundle_status` switches them off, so a partial owned by one bundle and
    included by another is a 500 waiting for the day somebody removes the first. ProcurementBundle's
    vendor address form includes this one.

    ## Why this exists at all

    A free-text province box accepts the COUNTRY code, and that is not a typo the application can
    absorb. `CA` is not a Canadian province, so a country-blind resolver falls through the Canadian
    table into the US one and resolves it to CALIFORNIA — a real province code, which therefore
    passes every "is the province known" guard and is then claimed by no Canadian calculator. The
    purchase order quotes $0.00 tax, and nothing on the document can tell that zero apart from a
    genuine one. Preventing the entry is worth more than any guard downstream of it.

    Parameters
    ----------
    kind        required  'country' or 'province'
    name        required  the input name, e.g. 'province' or 'warehouse_country'
    id          required  the element id — tests anchor to it (#627), and the <label> points at it
    selected    optional  the stored or submitted value; '' for a new row
    country     required for kind='province': the country code the list is scoped to
    target      required for kind='country':  the NAME of the province field this country scopes,
                          so a form holding two address blocks scopes each to its own partner
    required    optional  defaults to false
    label       optional  aria-label; defaults to 'Country' / 'Province or state'
    blank       optional  label for the empty option; defaults to '' (an empty option is always
                          rendered, so `required` has something to refuse)

    ## The stranded value, which is the trap a <select> INTRODUCES

    A select whose current value is not among its options renders with NOTHING selected, and the
    browser then posts the FIRST option. That is how opening a Draft product to fix a typo once
    silently ACTIVATED it. Here it would move a building to Alberta — and with it the tax on every
    future purchase order against it — because somebody edited its city.

    So a stored value the list does not offer is rendered as its own selected option rather than
    dropped: the row still reads, the admin can see what is actually stored, and nothing is rewritten
    behind them. It is labelled as not being in the list, because it is the thing they have to fix.
    Saving it again is REFUSED by name server-side; refusing loudly is the opposite of the silent
    substitution this guards against.

    An INACTIVE row resolves through province_name()/country_name(), which include inactive rows on
    purpose — a warehouse in a province you have stopped serving still shows that province's name
    rather than a bare code.

    ## No JavaScript

    `app.js` re-scopes the province list when the country changes, and only when the server rendered
    one option or fewer — so the options below are the server's, not the browser's. With scripting
    off there is no change event, and the re-scope happens on the round trip instead: the country
    that was submitted is what the next render scopes to, whether the save went through or was
    refused. That is the document forms' behaviour and this matches it.
#}
{% set regionSelected = (selected|default(''))|trim %}
{% set regionRequired = required|default(false) %}

{% if kind == 'country' %}
    {% set regionOptions = region_countries() %}
    {% set regionStranded = regionSelected != '' and regionSelected not in regionOptions|keys %}
    <select class="js-region-country" id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}"
            data-region-map="{{ region_map()|json_encode|e('html_attr') }}"
            data-province-target="{{ target }}"
            {% if regionRequired %}required{% endif %}
            aria-label="{{ label|default('Country') }}">
        <option value="">{{ blank|default('') }}</option>
        {% if regionStranded %}
            {# Kept and SELECTED rather than dropped — see the note above. #}
            <option value="{{ regionSelected }}" selected>{{ country_name(regionSelected) }} — not in the list</option>
        {% endif %}
        {% for code, countryLabel in regionOptions %}
            <option value="{{ code }}" {{ regionSelected == code ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ countryLabel }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
    </select>
{% else %}
    {% set regionCountry = (country|default(''))|trim %}
    {% set regionOptions = region_provinces(regionCountry) %}
    {% set regionStranded = regionSelected != '' and regionSelected not in regionOptions|keys %}
    <select class="js-region-province" id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}"
            data-province-name="{{ name }}"
            data-selected="{{ regionSelected }}"
            {% if regionRequired %}required{% endif %}
            aria-label="{{ label|default('Province or state') }}">
        <option value="">{{ blank|default('') }}</option>
        {% if regionStranded %}
            <option value="{{ regionSelected }}" selected>
                {{- province_name(regionCountry, regionSelected) }} — not a province or state of
                {{ regionCountry != '' ? country_name(regionCountry) : 'the selected country' -}}
            </option>
        {% endif %}
        {% for code, provinceLabel in regionOptions %}
            <option value="{{ code }}" {{ regionSelected == code ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ provinceLabel }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
    </select>
{% endif %}
